    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 062240\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n340 PM PDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* A band of showers with a chance of embedded thunderstorms \n  continues along the I-15 and I-40 corridors this afternoon.\n\n* Drier conditions expected for Labor Day, with low chances for \n  isolated storm activity later this week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Saturday.\n\nOur band of light to moderate rain has set up along the I-15 and I-\n40 corridors. Rain rates have generally been around 0.10\" per hour \nor less, with rainfall totals across the area ranging from a trace \nto 0.33\" as of 1230PM. Have seen only a few lightning strikes so \nfar, with best odds of additional strikes on the northern edge of \nthe precip band. Barring any more robust convective development, \nanticipating rainfall remain light to moderate. Activity is \nexpected to wane this evening once the shortwave moves through. \nClouds and precip will keep temperatures well below normal for \nearly September. Highs will range from the upper 70s to low 90s \nacross the area, with the values on the higher end likely being \n\"midnight highs\".\n\nAs the Pacific trough lifts northward tomorrow, we lose the forcing \nand some of the moisture that helped produce the widespread rain \ntoday. We'll still have some high clouds around, but drier \nconditions return along with seasonable temperatures. Ensemble \nguidance continues to show a ridge retrograding back into the Desert \nSouthwest later this week, promoting a warming trend through at \nleast Thursday. By that time, the forecast calls for highs 3-7 \ndegrees above normal, with values eclipsing the century mark over \nmost of the Mojave Desert. However, guidance also keeps modest \nmonsoonal moisture in place, allowing low (less than 30%) \nshower/storm chances to linger over the higher terrain. A \nweakening of the ridge and transition to more zonal flow next \nweekend should scour moisture out and bring temperatures down \nslightly.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Light \nrain showers will move out of the Las Vegas area this evening. \nSouthwesterly winds with peak gusts between 25 and 30 knots will \nalso weaken after sunset, maintaining a southwesterly direction with \nspeeds under 10 knots. Light northeasterly winds may develop again \ntomorrow morning before gusty westerly to southwesterly winds return \nin the afternoon. Temperatures remain under 100 degrees through \nTuesday. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...The band of rain showers \ncurrently over the area will dissipate after sunset. Gusty northerly \nwinds in the Colorado River Valley and southwesterly winds in the \nLas Vegas Valley should also weaken at that time. Winds should \nmostly follow a diurnal pattern starting tonight including gusty \nwesterly winds in the Las Vegas Valley. Mid and high clouds \ngradually clear out. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
